# Build output. A payload builds beside its project; nothing built belongs in the repository.
out/
obj/
*.obj
*.pdb
*.ilk
*.exp
*.idb
*.tlog
*.log
*.binlog

# Visual Studio local state, which is per-machine by design.
.vs/
*.user
*.suo

# Python bytecode, from the Python payload lane.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]

# What the installer downloads. The bundle is a release asset, fetched and verified at install
# time - a 26 MB binary in git is a 26 MB binary in every fork, forever.
*.zip
downloads/

# The headers and libraries a native payload builds against. THEY ARE RELEASE ARTIFACTS, exactly
# like the plugin binaries, and they arrive with the release rather than living in this repository.
#
# Two reasons, and the second matters more. Committing a .lib repeats the mistake above - every fork
# carries it forever. And a header committed here would version INDEPENDENTLY of the plugin you have
# installed, so the contract you compile against and the binary you call could disagree while both
# looked current. Shipping them together makes that impossible: the headers on your disk are the
# ones that came with the binary on your disk.
#
# The README in each directory is tracked, because the contract is ours to state even when the
# contents are not ours to commit.
include/*
!include/README.md
lib/*
!lib/README.md

# Windows and editor litter.
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
*.swp
*~
